Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:23:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] libfprint: add a driver for the Betterlife BF63112

This series adds a read-only driver for the Betterlife BF63112 fingerprint
module, the sensor on the ONEXPLAYER 3 (ACPI\BF63112 on the Intel LPSS SPI
bus).  It is based on libfprint v1.94.9 (commit dc8b05f0a30e) and builds and
passes the in-tree unit tests there, but it has not run against the sensor yet.

The device is match-in-sensor and speaks a framed SPI protocol recovered from
static analysis of the vendor's Windows UMDF driver.  This first series is
deliberately limited: it binds the device, queries sensor information (0xC1)
and firmware version (0xA0), and implements open, close, suspend and resume.
Enrol, verify and identify return FP_DEVICE_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED because the
0xB0/0xB1 payloads, the template handle, the score encoding and the
SM2/SM3/SM4 session are not decoded.  No write opcode is allowlisted, so
templates already enrolled from Windows cannot be touched.

Patch 1 adds the dependency-free protocol, SPI/SPB transport, _CRS and
discovery helpers.  Patch 2 adds the FpDevice driver and wires it into the
build and the generated udev rule.  Patch 3 adds a documentation stub.

Known limitations, all repeated in the commit messages and the docs stub:
no hardware transcript; the _CRS bus parameters are unread; the answer
framing is assumed to mirror the request; the sensor and firmware field
layouts are inferred; and the interrupt line is handled by polling because
the SPI controller driver does not expose it.  These must be resolved, or
explicitly accepted by the maintainers, before the driver can be considered
complete.

The licence line is a TODO in every new file.  The project owner has not
chosen a licence for this subtree yet; the SPDX-License-Identifier
(LGPL-2.1-or-later, to match libfprint) is set before this series is submitted
upstream.

Build and test as posted: meson setup with -Ddrivers=bf63112 (introspection
and API docs off), ninja, then meson test.  The new files compile without
warnings; the two in-tree unit tests pass and the driver-emulation tests skip
because they need introspection/umockdev.

